Robin Johnson wrote:
>
> The trick here is either to attack from outside (as Marky suggested),

The trick is to measure your range with missiles and do the killing
from that range.  And since the priest can move to either side of
his altar you'll need to cut a hole in the temple wall at least 3
squares wide.  And start firing from the shortest range of any of
the weapons types you try but definitely outside the temple.  Out
of melee range of the priest and if possible out of lightning bolt
range.  Just in case, drop all wands and rings and such before
you start your barrage.

Fourteen unenchanted daggers isn't going to be enough.  Get every
missile you've yet seen in the dungeon.  Every dagger no matter
its condition (cursed daggers don't weld to yout hands when
thrown), every dart, every arrow, every spear, every rock.  Yes,
every rock.  If you throw every weapon missile at the priest and
(s)he's still alive, it sucks to have to go get every rock in the
dungeon to try again and discover the priest is fully healed by the
time you return and you can't get to your missiles without suffering
melee with that powerfull priest.  If you are left standing on a pile
of arrows and rocks and such looking at a dead priest, that doesn't
suck.

> or to make it your temple first by converting the altar.

Extremely bad idea because that means melee with a priest
well stocked with hit points and spells.  Especially bad idea if
you give in to the temptation to use the priest to convert the
altar until you have a plan to deal with the resulting demon.

> However, priests are much more useful alive.

That's a matter of tastes.  I can get more AC points from a
coaligned altar than every priest in the game can sell me.

> That, and wanting a robe, *might* have
> constituted a good reason to kill the priest, but I still didn't.

I always want a robe, even if it will only be a backup item.

And as a chaotic, I always want Stormy as well so a coaligned
altar becomes worth that priest's life.



